"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including the preoperative PSA of 15 and the pathological T-stage of pT3a with extraprostatic extension. Positive lymphovascular invasion was noted, which contributes to risk assessment. Countervailing findings include negative surgical margins and no documented seminal vesicle invasion. The absence of removed lymph nodes widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. Biopsy findings (Gleason 3+3/3+4) differ from the prostatectomy grade (Gleason 3+4), though both indicate adenocarcinoma. Prognostic revision would be significantly aided by postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory, as this information is currently unknown. The current data suggests a moderate risk profile given the combination of factors. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 40.81–84.77 months; its coverage has not been validated."
